WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.270
SPEAKER: Determining the right
price for your retail products

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.040
is a difficult task.

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:08.070
Price items too high, and
you might lose business.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:11.770
Price them too low, and you
lower your profit margin.

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:15.000
So how do you incorporate
different data sources

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.670
to analyze historical
pricing trends

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:20.610
and make predictions
on future demand?

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:23.910
Today we'll show you how
using Google's data cloud.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:26.685
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.800
There are lots of
different data sources

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.150
that can help you understand
how to optimally price items.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:42.180
You might have information
on transactions,

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:46.380
details on products, like
categories and list prices,

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:49.740
and data on the
customers themselves.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:53.100
However, the data required
to drive pricing insight

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:57.030
is often disparate, potentially
coming from different systems

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:59.460
with their own formats.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:03.120
One way to make working with
these sources more effective

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.960
is to combine key attributes
into a single cleaned

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.740
and standardized schema, which
we'll refer to as the Common

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.400
Data Model, or CDM.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.700
In this tutorial, we'll
use Dataprep by Trifecta

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.820
to standardize data coming
from different sources

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:25.110
and unify the necessary
fields in the CDM table, which

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:28.770
will live in BigQuery, our
fully managed, scalable data

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:30.930
warehouse.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:33.720
Once data is formatted
for analysis,

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.500
we'll show how analysts can
explore historical trends

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:42.010
to understand the impact
of prior pricing changes.

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:45.180
Additionally, we'll use
BigQuery's machine learning

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:48.360
capabilities to create
predictive models that

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.150
estimate future sales.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.970
Finally, we'll use the
output of these models

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:57.990
within a Looker dashboard to
create what-if scenarios, where

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:00.510
business users can
analyze what sales

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:03.640
might look like with
certain price changes.

00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:07.050
So let's jump in.

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:10.560
Inside of Dataprep,
you create flows, which

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.140
represent a data pipeline.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.150
Within this flow,
we have connected

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.220
each of our data sets.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.580
In this case, we're using
Google Sheets and a table

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:24.330
containing our customer
descriptions in BigQuery.

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:27.550
But you can connect a variety
of different data sources,

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:30.990
including flat files,
transactional databases,

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:34.320
and SaaS tools, like Salesforce.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.780
You might notice that
we have one transaction

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.660
file for each month,
representing the orders that

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.860
were placed in that period.

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:46.290
The first thing you'll want
to do is combine those files.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:50.460
In Dataprep, you use recipes
to transform and join data

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.500
together.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.640
Opening this recipe, you can
see that there are actually

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.950
two things happening.

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:03.180
First, we're unioning or
combining the transactions data

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:06.540
into one single set.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.020
But then we're standardizing
customer descriptions.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:13.290
This is because the
names of customers

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:16.740
are sometimes misspelled
or represented differently

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:18.810
across transactions.

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:22.320
But with Dataprep, you can
use a fuzzy matching algorithm

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.010
to standardize customer
names with similar strings

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:29.850
where values with characters in
common are clustered together

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:33.660
or pronunciation where
values that sound alike

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:35.430
are clustered together.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:40.650
This way, we make sure customers
are represented consistently.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:42.600
Outside of the
transactions recipe,

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.200
we have other recipes to
calculate accurate pricing

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.810
using functions that
pivot, aggregate,

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:54.120
and join to customer data, and
a recipe that automatically

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.640
reformats the prepared
data structure

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.360
to match the existing
CDM table schema,

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:04.900
using Dataprep's rapid
target functionality.

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.760
Finally, the CDM is
pushed into BigQuery.

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:12.180
And not only can we
manually run this flow,

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:16.260
but we can also set it to
run on a scheduled basis,

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:20.250
so the CDM table
is always fresh.

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.980
With our prepared
data in BigQuery,

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:24.990
we're ready to take
a look at what's

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:29.460
happened historically when we've
changed the price of an item.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:33.840
Here, we're using standard SQL
to create a temporary table

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.350
with one row for
each price change,

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:39.610
and it contains
relevant information,

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.090
like the total number of
orders at the current price

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:46.410
point compared to orders at
the previous price point.

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:49.680
With this temp table in place,
we can do things like look

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:52.740
at the average
price change or see

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:57.060
how sales have changed relative
to the price difference.

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:00.300
Now that we have historical
analysis squared away,

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.910
let's incorporate machine
learning to predict

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:06.060
what will happen in the future.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:09.450
With BigQueryML, we can
build an ARIMA model,

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:11.610
one of the most
widely used approaches

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:15.540
for statistical forecasting
with time series.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:17.610
And this will use
historical sales

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:21.360
for each product to predict
the future demand, all

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:23.190
in just a few lines of SQL.

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:25.390
How cool is that?

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:28.200
So using BigQuery, we're
able to draw insights

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:31.290
from huge amounts
of data in no time.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:33.930
But to empower business
decision makers,

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:39.190
we want to create a report
accessible by the broader team.

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:42.000
This is where Looker
comes into play.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.670
With Looker, we
create a data model

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:47.940
using Looker's
semantic layer, LookML,

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:52.770
that stores the definitions
of our metrics using SQL.

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:55.680
Looker uses this model
to compile and send

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:01.320
SQL queries back to BigQuery on
behalf of non-technical users.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:04.410
This means we take full
advantage of the power

00:06:04.410 --> 00:06:07.410
and intelligence
that BigQuery offers,

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:10.050
all while giving business
users the ability

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:13.560
to explore governed data
without needing an analyst

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:16.560
to write SQL for them.

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:19.140
With our LookML
model in place, we

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:23.820
can build out a dashboard to
influence pricing decisions.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:28.800
Here, we've built a report that
services historical trends,

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.100
building off of that temp
table we created earlier.

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:36.060
And because Looker
compiles SQL on the fly,

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:41.790
we can drill all the way to
SKU level detail from any tile.

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:43.770
Scrolling down on
the report, we've

00:06:43.770 --> 00:06:47.220
also created a what-if
tool where decision makers

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:49.560
can see what might
happen if they

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:52.750
change the price of products.

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:55.200
For example, if
you wanted to see

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:57.960
what would happen if the
order volume went down

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:02.430
to 75% of the predicted
value and pricing

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:06.690
across all products
was raised by 25%.

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:09.880
And the best part, this
is all just the beginning.

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:12.150
There are lots more ways
that Google Cloud can

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:14.760
help you optimize
your retail pricing,

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:17.700
like leveraging geospatial
analytics features

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:22.020
to see how the proximity of
competitors impacts sales

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:24.990
or incorporating
publicly available data,

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:28.570
like weather and search
trends into your analysis.

00:07:28.570 --> 00:07:30.240
So if you're interested
in learning more

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:32.220
about Google's data
cloud, then make

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:34.410
sure to check out
the links below,

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:37.140
including a step-by-step
tutorial of what

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:38.160
we showed today.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:41.210
[MUSIC PLAYING]

